{
  "id": "COMPOUND:42472 - PROTEIN:5940",
  "verbalization": "The visible evidence suggests a potential relationship between N-Methyltyramine and Carboxylesterase 2 through a series of intermediate targets. N-Methyltyramine is directly linked to Dehydrogenase/reductase SDR family member 9, which in turn is targeted by multiple compounds, including Flavipin, Dihydrostreptomycin Sulfate, and Sasanquasaponin I. All of these compounds are predicted to have a target of Carboxylesterase 2. This indicates that N-Methyltyramine may act through a common pathway, where it influences the activity of the SDR family member 9, which then serves as a mediator in the interaction with Carboxylesterase 2. The evidence suggests that N-Methyltyramine may modulate the activity of Carboxylesterase 2 indirectly, possibly through the SDR family member 9, which is a key intermediate in the pathway. The integration of these findings highlights a potential mechanism where N-Methyltyramine could influence Carboxylesterase 2 via a series of targets, emphasizing the role of the SDR family in mediating this interaction.",
